From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Darren Shepherd <darren@obot.ai>
Date: Thu, 6 Aug 2026 21:51:27 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] perf(control): throttle the forced render, 4.2 -> 28 MB/s

A ghostty pane drained its pty at 4.2 MB/s where cascadia managed 38.1. The
whole gap was one line: _connectionOutputHandler forced a synchronous
render_now on *every* chunk of pty output, on the connection's output thread -
the thread whose blocking is the backpressure to the child. So every chunk paid
for a full rebuild-and-present before the next could be read.

Measured on an 8 MB corpus, best of several runs, Release + ReleaseFast, nothing
else compositing:

  render per chunk (before)         4.2 MB/s
  throttled, leading+trailing 8ms  12.6 MB/s
  throttled, trailing-only 8ms     28.0 MB/s median, 34.0 best
  no forced render at all          37.4 MB/s
  cascadia, same corpus            38.1 MB/s

Trailing-only matters and the intermediate number is why it is recorded: a
leading invocation runs *inline on the calling thread*, so it goes on blocking
the backpressure path once per interval and recovers only a third of the gap.
Trailing alone moves every render onto the timer thread. The cost is that the
first frame of a burst is 8ms late, which is not perceptible.

What the forced render is for is unchanged and still load-bearing: libghostty's
wakeup coalesces, so a notify landing after the render thread sampled state can
be folded into a wake that has already finished, and the last batch never
reaches the screen. The trailing edge is exactly that final render. Not
debounced, so a sustained flood still gets a frame every interval rather than
being starved.

The remaining ~25% is the cost of forcing renders at all. The proper fix is
upstream in ghostty - a wakeup that re-arms when a notify arrives mid-wake would
remove the need for this entirely - and is Phase 7 design work, not this change.

**The freeze this guards against needs a human to confirm**: no automated check
in this repo covers it (docs/manual-validation.md item 1), because the harness
has its own paint pump and never reproduced it.

WT suites: terminalCore 54/54, unitSettingsModel 159/159, unitControl 62/62,
terminalApp 51/51.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01BgDGqEqRBB8L5Ueoq1uuxy
---
 .../TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp    | 48 ++++++++++++++++++-
 .../TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.h      |  3 ++
 2 files changed, 50 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp b/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp
index bd3575d51..90d24eaee 100644
--- a/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp
+++ b/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.cpp
@@ -80,6 +80,24 @@ namespace winrt::Microsoft::Terminal::Control::implementation
 
         _initializedTerminal = true;
 
+        // Bounds how often output can force a frame. 8ms is two frames at 60Hz
+        // and one at 120Hz - fast enough that output looks continuous, slow
+        // enough that a flood is not paying for a synchronous present per
+        // chunk of pty output.
+        _renderThrottler = std::make_unique<til::throttled_func<>>(
+            til::throttled_func_options{
+                .delay = std::chrono::milliseconds{ 8 },
+                .debounce = false,
+                .leading = false,
+                .trailing = true,
+            },
+            [weakThis = get_weak()]() {
+                if (const auto self = weakThis.get())
+                {
+                    self->_renderNow();
+                }
+            });
+
         // If the renderer already built a swap chain while we were in
         // ghostty_surface_new, its action fired before anything could act on
         // it. Raising once here costs nothing when the handle is still 0.
@@ -195,6 +213,10 @@ namespace winrt::Microsoft::Terminal::Control::implementation
         }
         _closing = true;
 
+        // Before the surface goes: its destructor cancels any pending trailing
+        // render, which would otherwise fire against a freed surface.
+        _renderThrottler.reset();
+
         _connectionOutputRevoker.revoke();
         _connectionStateRevoker.revoke();
 
@@ -317,7 +339,31 @@ namespace winrt::Microsoft::Terminal::Control::implementation
         // A pane in a XAML SwapChainPanel has no paint loop, so nothing else
         // ever forces this. hwnd-host does it on WM_PAINT, which is why three
         // phases of harness work never surfaced the problem.
-        ghostty_surface_render_now(_surface);
+        //
+        // Throttled, though, because forcing it on *every* chunk cost a factor
+        // of nine. This handler runs on the connection's output thread and
+        // render_now is synchronous, so a forced render per chunk put a full
+        // rebuild-and-present in the middle of the path whose blocking is the
+        // backpressure to the child. Measured on an 8 MB corpus: 4.2 MB/s with
+        // a render per chunk, 37.4 MB/s without one, against cascadia's 38.1.
+        //
+        // leading + trailing, not debounced:
+        //   * trailing only, no leading - a leading invocation runs *inline on
+        //                this thread*, so it would go on blocking the
+        //                backpressure path once per interval. Measured:
+        //                leading+trailing at 8ms gave 12.6 MB/s. The cost of
+        //                dropping it is that the first frame of a burst is 8ms
+        //                late, which is not perceptible.
+        //   * trailing - the last chunk always draws, which is the whole point
+        //                of forcing renders here. Without it the coalescing
+        //                wakeup can drop the final batch and the pane freezes
+        //                mid-output, which is the defect this code exists for.
+        //   * not debounced - a sustained flood must not starve the screen;
+        //                every interval gets a frame.
+        if (_renderThrottler)
+        {
+            (*_renderThrottler)();
+        }
     }
 
     void GhosttyControlCore::_connectionStateChangedHandler()
diff --git a/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.h b/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.h
index 93c9863cc..314370487 100644
--- a/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.h
+++ b/src/cascadia/TerminalControl/GhosttyControlCore.h
@@ -334,6 +334,9 @@ namespace winrt::Microsoft::Terminal::Control::implementation
         // so Search() starts the work and returns the last counts that arrived.
         // -1 is not a placeholder: SearchBoxControl renders a negative total as
         // "Searching...", which is exactly what is true until they do.
+        // Bounds how often pty output forces a frame; see _connectionOutputHandler.
+        std::unique_ptr<til::throttled_func<>> _renderThrottler;
+
         std::wstring _searchNeedle;
         int32_t _searchTotal{ -1 };
         int32_t _searchSelected{ -1 };
